Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:08.20,Title,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,445)\fad(600,500)\bord3\blur0.5}ODYSSEY — BOOK XIV
Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:08.20,Deck,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,565)\fad(600,500)}The hut of Eumaeus: the slave's welcome, the second Cretan tale, the cloak story.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.20,0:28:40.25,Header,,0,0,0,,{\fad(800,500)}HOMER’S ODYSSEY  ·  BOOK XIV
Dialogue: 1,0:00:08.20,0:00:13.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But he went up from the harbor by a rough track\Nthrough wooded country, along the heights, where Athena
Dialogue: 1,0:00:13.76,0:00:17.06,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}had shown him the noble swineherd — the man who cared
Dialogue: 1,0:00:17.06,0:00:21.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for his substance beyond all the\Nhouse-slaves noble Odysseus had acquired.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:21.50,0:00:23.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He found him sitting in the fore-court, where his yard
Dialogue: 1,0:00:23.82,0:00:27.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}was built up high, in a place\Nwith a clear view all around,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:27.64,0:00:31.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}big and handsome, with a cleared\Nrun round it. The swineherd
Dialogue: 1,0:00:31.54,0:00:37.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}had built it himself for the swine of his absent master —\Nwithout his mistress, without old Laertes —
Dialogue: 1,0:00:37.64,0:00:41.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}out of quarried stones, and coped it with wild pear;
Dialogue: 1,0:00:41.26,0:00:45.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and outside he had driven stakes the\Nwhole circuit, this way and that,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:45.02,0:00:50.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}close-set and thick, split out of the black heart of\Nthe oak; and inside the yard he had made twelve sties
Dialogue: 1,0:00:50.86,0:00:56.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}hard by one another, bedding for the swine;\Nand in each one fifty wallowing sows were penned,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:56.56,0:01:03.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}breeding females. The boars slept outside,\Nfar fewer: for the godlike suitors kept thinning them
Dialogue: 1,0:01:03.12,0:01:07.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}at their feasts — the swineherd forever\Nsending in the best of all the fatted hogs;
Dialogue: 1,0:01:07.80,0:01:10.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and these numbered three hundred and sixty.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:10.14,0:01:16.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And beside them four dogs always slept — like wild\Nbeasts, raised by the swineherd, a leader of men.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:16.60,0:01:21.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He himself was fitting sandals to his feet,\Ncutting them from a well-tanned oxhide. The others
Dialogue: 1,0:01:21.98,0:01:26.72,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}had gone their ways, this man here,\Nthat man there, with the herded swine —
Dialogue: 1,0:01:26.72,0:01:29.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}three of them; the fourth he had sent off to the city
Dialogue: 1,0:01:29.20,0:01:32.68,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to bring a hog to the overweening suitors — forced to it —
Dialogue: 1,0:01:32.68,0:01:35.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}so they might slaughter it and\Nglut their hearts with meat.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:35.76,0:01:38.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Suddenly the baying dogs caught sight of Odysseus:
Dialogue: 1,0:01:38.46,0:01:45.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}they rushed him, yelping. And Odysseus sat down —\Npure craft — and the staff dropped from his hand.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:45.18,0:01:48.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}There, at his own farm,\Nhe would have suffered shameful hurt,
Dialogue: 1,0:01:48.32,0:01:51.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}but the swineherd came fast on his quick feet after them,
Dialogue: 1,0:01:51.38,0:01:54.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}darting through the gateway —\Nthe hide flew from his hand —
Dialogue: 1,0:01:54.64,0:02:00.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and shouting he scattered the dogs this way and that\Nwith a shower of stones. And he addressed his master:
Dialogue: 1,0:02:00.66,0:02:03.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Old man — the dogs came one breath short of tearing you
Dialogue: 1,0:02:03.54,0:02:06.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to pieces, and the shame of it would have poured on me.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:06.66,0:02:10.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And the gods have given me griefs\Nenough besides, and groaning:
Dialogue: 1,0:02:10.30,0:02:14.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}here I sit, mourning a godlike master,\Nand fatten hogs for other men
Dialogue: 1,0:02:14.18,0:02:20.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to eat, while he, perhaps, is starving for bread and\Nwanders the lands and towns of men of alien speech —
Dialogue: 1,0:02:20.32,0:02:23.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}if indeed he lives still, and sees the light of the sun.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:23.58,0:02:28.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But follow me — into the hut, old man, so that you too,\Nwhen you have had your fill of bread and wine,
Dialogue: 1,0:02:28.56,0:02:32.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}may say where you come from,\Nand all the cares you have borne."
Dialogue: 1,0:02:32.22,0:02:35.16,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So speaking the noble swineherd led him to the hut,
Dialogue: 1,0:02:35.16,0:02:38.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}brought him in and set him down:\Nhe heaped springy brushwood
Dialogue: 1,0:02:38.52,0:02:44.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and spread on it the skin of a shaggy wild goat —\Nhis own bedding, big and thick. And Odysseus was glad
Dialogue: 1,0:02:44.66,0:02:48.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}that he received him so, and spoke a word, and named him:
Dialogue: 1,0:02:48.54,0:02:51.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"May Zeus grant you, stranger,\Nand the other deathless gods,
Dialogue: 1,0:02:51.34,0:02:55.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}whatever you wish most — since you\Nwelcomed me with all your heart."
Dialogue: 1,0:02:55.36,0:02:58.04,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:
Dialogue: 1,0:02:58.04,0:03:01.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Stranger, it is not right for me —\Nnot though a worse man came —
Dialogue: 1,0:03:01.26,0:03:05.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to slight a stranger:\Nfor from Zeus they come, all of them,
Dialogue: 1,0:03:05.66,0:03:08.72,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}strangers and beggars; and a gift, though small, is dear —
Dialogue: 1,0:03:08.72,0:03:13.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}from such as we are: for that is the way of slaves,\Nalways afraid, when the masters who hold the power
Dialogue: 1,0:03:13.98,0:03:18.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}are the young ones. As for him —\Nthe gods have bound his homecoming,
Dialogue: 1,0:03:18.28,0:03:21.78,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the man who would have loved me devotedly,\Nand given me possessions —
Dialogue: 1,0:03:21.78,0:03:25.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a house, a plot of land,\Na wife that many men had courted —
Dialogue: 1,0:03:25.50,0:03:28.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}such things as a kind-hearted master gives his house-slave
Dialogue: 1,0:03:28.08,0:03:31.68,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}who has labored hard for him,\Nwhose work a god makes prosper,
Dialogue: 1,0:03:31.68,0:03:35.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the way this work of mine prospers,\Nat which I hold my post.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:35.36,0:03:38.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}For that my master would have paid\Nme richly, had he grown old here.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:38.82,0:03:43.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But he is dead. — And I wish\NHelen's whole breed had gone down
Dialogue: 1,0:03:43.60,0:03:48.06,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}on its knees to destruction,\Nfor she loosed the knees of many men:
Dialogue: 1,0:03:48.06,0:03:55.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}he too went off for the sake of Agamemnon's honor\Nto Ilium of the fine horses, to fight the Trojans."
Dialogue: 1,0:03:55.30,0:03:58.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So speaking he quickly belted in his tunic with his sash
Dialogue: 1,0:03:58.34,0:04:02.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and went to the sties, where the\Ntribes of young pigs were penned.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:02.36,0:04:04.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He took two out of there, and slaughtered them both,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:04.84,0:04:08.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}singed them, cut them small, and threaded them on spits,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:08.30,0:04:11.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and when he had roasted all of it,\Nhe brought and set it by Odysseus
Dialogue: 1,0:04:11.60,0:04:15.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}hot on the spits themselves,\Nand dusted it with white barley,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:15.42,0:04:21.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and mixed honey-sweet wine in an ivy-wood bowl,\Nand sat down facing him, and pressed him on:
Dialogue: 1,0:04:21.56,0:04:24.48,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Eat now, stranger — what house-slaves have to offer:
Dialogue: 1,0:04:24.48,0:04:28.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}young pig. The fatted hogs — the suitors eat those,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:28.26,0:04:32.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}with no thought in their hearts of\Nthe gods' watching eye, no mercy.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:32.20,0:04:35.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Yet the blessed gods love no ruthless dealing:
Dialogue: 1,0:04:35.08,0:04:37.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}they honor justice, and the right acts of men.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:37.74,0:04:43.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Why — even enemies, even raiders, who land on\Na foreign coast, and Zeus grants them plunder,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:43.12,0:04:48.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and they fill their ships and set off home — even on\Ntheir hearts falls the strong fear of the watching eye.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:48.82,0:04:52.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But these men — these men know something;\Nthey have heard some god's own voice
Dialogue: 1,0:04:52.74,0:04:55.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}telling his grim death: that is why they will not woo
Dialogue: 1,0:04:55.32,0:04:58.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}in the right way, nor go home\Nto their own, but at their ease
Dialogue: 1,0:04:58.62,0:05:02.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}they devour the goods, high-handed, and spare nothing.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:02.18,0:05:08.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}For every night and every day that comes from Zeus,\Nthey slaughter victims — never one, and never only two —
Dialogue: 1,0:05:08.58,0:05:11.72,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and wine they draw off past all measure, and waste it.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:11.72,0:05:17.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}For his livelihood was past speaking. No hero\Nanywhere had so much — not on the dark mainland,
Dialogue: 1,0:05:17.86,0:05:23.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}not on Ithaca itself; no twenty men together have\Nwealth so great. I will count it through for you:
Dialogue: 1,0:05:23.84,0:05:27.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}twelve herds of cattle on the mainland;\Nas many flocks of sheep;
Dialogue: 1,0:05:27.44,0:05:30.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}as many droves of pigs; as many ranging herds of goats
Dialogue: 1,0:05:30.60,0:05:33.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}in the care of foreigners and of his own herdsmen there.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:33.84,0:05:36.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And here, at the island's end, wide-ranging herds of goats
Dialogue: 1,0:05:36.76,0:05:40.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}graze, eleven in all, with good men watching over them,
Dialogue: 1,0:05:40.26,0:05:43.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and every day each man of them\Ndrives up a beast for those others —
Dialogue: 1,0:05:43.74,0:05:48.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}whichever of the fatted goats looks best;\Nand I keep guard over these sows, and protect them,
Dialogue: 1,0:05:48.70,0:05:52.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and choose out the best of the swine, and send it in."
Dialogue: 1,0:05:52.02,0:05:55.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So he spoke; and Odysseus ate his meat and drank his wine,
Dialogue: 1,0:05:55.80,0:05:58.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}ravenous, silent — planting evils for the suitors.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:58.90,0:06:04.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But when he had supped, and steadied his heart with food,\Nthe swineherd filled the cup he drank from himself
Dialogue: 1,0:06:04.02,0:06:08.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}brim-full of wine and gave it;\Nand he took it, glad at heart,
Dialogue: 1,0:06:08.20,0:06:11.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to him:
Dialogue: 1,0:06:11.08,0:06:16.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Friend — who was the man who bought you with his goods,\Nthe master rich and powerful as you describe?
Dialogue: 1,0:06:16.62,0:06:19.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}You say he perished for the sake of Agamemnon's honor:
Dialogue: 1,0:06:19.38,0:06:23.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}name him — I may know him, being such a one as he was.
Dialogue: 1,0:06:23.14,0:06:26.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}For Zeus knows, I suppose, and the other deathless gods,
Dialogue: 1,0:06:26.40,0:06:29.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}whether I have seen him. I have wandered far."
Dialogue: 1,0:06:29.58,0:06:32.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then the swineherd answered him, a leader of men:
Dialogue: 1,0:06:32.58,0:06:38.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Old man — no wanderer coming with news of him\Ncould persuade his wife now, or his dear son.
Dialogue: 1,0:06:38.26,0:06:42.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Drifters in need of keep — they lie at random,\Nand have no wish to speak the truth.
Dialogue: 1,0:06:42.90,0:06:48.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Every vagrant who finds his way to the land of\NIthaca goes to my mistress and babbles his deceits;
Dialogue: 1,0:06:48.26,0:06:52.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and she receives him kindly,\Nand treats him well, and questions everything,
Dialogue: 1,0:06:52.62,0:06:55.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and as she grieves the tears come falling from her lids,
Dialogue: 1,0:06:55.64,0:06:59.06,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}as is the right way of a wife whose husband dies far off.
Dialogue: 1,0:06:59.06,0:07:02.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}You too, old man, would have a\Nstory stitched together fast enough
Dialogue: 1,0:07:02.80,0:07:06.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}if someone would give you clothing — a cloak and a tunic.
Dialogue: 1,0:07:06.12,0:07:08.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But him — by now the dogs and the quick birds
Dialogue: 1,0:07:08.66,0:07:12.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}have torn the skin from his bones,\Nand the life has gone out of him;
Dialogue: 1,0:07:12.18,0:07:15.24,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}or the fish have eaten him in the sea, and his bones
Dialogue: 1,0:07:15.24,0:07:19.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}lie on a shore somewhere, wrapped deep in drifting sand.
Dialogue: 1,0:07:19.50,0:07:23.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So he is dead and gone over there,\Nand grief is left behind
Dialogue: 1,0:07:23.08,0:07:30.78,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for all who loved him — for me above all: for never\Nagain will I find a master so gentle, wherever I go —
Dialogue: 1,0:07:30.78,0:07:36.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}not though I came again to my father's and my mother's\Nhouse, where I was born, and where they reared me.
Dialogue: 1,0:07:36.98,0:07:40.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And it is not even for them I\Nmourn so much, for all my longing
Dialogue: 1,0:07:40.46,0:07:43.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to see them with my eyes again in my own country:
Dialogue: 1,0:07:43.46,0:07:47.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}no — it is longing for Odysseus,\Nwho is gone, that takes hold of me.
Dialogue: 1,0:07:47.80,0:07:51.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I shrink from naming him, stranger, even now he is absent,
Dialogue: 1,0:07:51.12,0:07:54.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for he loved me beyond measure, and cared for me at heart.
Dialogue: 1,0:07:54.40,0:07:57.78,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I call him 'my beloved lord,' even far away."
Dialogue: 1,0:07:57.78,0:08:01.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then much-enduring noble Odysseus spoke to him again:
Dialogue: 1,0:08:01.10,0:08:07.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Friend — since you deny it flat, and say that he will\Ncome no more, and your heart is set in disbelief —
Dialogue: 1,0:08:07.12,0:08:10.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}then I will say it not just so, but with an oath upon it:
Dialogue: 1,0:08:10.28,0:08:16.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Odysseus is coming home. And let my reward for the\Ngood news come the moment he arrives at his own halls:
Dialogue: 1,0:08:16.56,0:08:19.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}dress me then in a cloak and a tunic, fine clothing.
Dialogue: 1,0:08:19.88,0:08:23.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Before that — though I stand in\Nsore need — I will take nothing.
Dialogue: 1,0:08:23.94,0:08:30.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}For hateful to me as the very gates of Hades is\Nthe man who yields to poverty and babbles deceits.
Dialogue: 1,0:08:30.52,0:08:35.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Now Zeus be my witness, first of the gods,\Nand this table of friendship,
Dialogue: 1,0:08:35.02,0:08:38.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and the hearth of blameless Odysseus,\Nto which I have come:
Dialogue: 1,0:08:38.34,0:08:41.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}all this shall be accomplished exactly as I say.
Dialogue: 1,0:08:41.88,0:08:45.48,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Within this very turning of the\Nlight Odysseus will be here:
Dialogue: 1,0:08:45.48,0:08:50.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}between this month's waning and the rising of the next,\Nhe will come home; and he will punish whoever
Dialogue: 1,0:08:50.84,0:08:54.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}dishonors his wife and his shining son in this place."
Dialogue: 1,0:08:54.56,0:08:57.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:
Dialogue: 1,0:08:57.46,0:09:00.68,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Old man, I will never be paying\Nthat reward for good news,
Dialogue: 1,0:09:00.68,0:09:03.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and Odysseus will come home no more. Drink your wine
Dialogue: 1,0:09:03.64,0:09:06.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}in peace, and let us turn our\Nminds to other things. Do not
Dialogue: 1,0:09:06.88,0:09:12.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}remind me of all this: for the heart in my breast\Naches whenever anyone recalls my faithful master.
Dialogue: 1,0:09:12.46,0:09:17.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}As for the oath — we will let it be. But may\NOdysseus come — as I wish it, as Penelope wishes,
Dialogue: 1,0:09:17.96,0:09:21.04,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and old Laertes, and godlike Telemachus.
Dialogue: 1,0:09:21.04,0:09:24.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And now it is the boy I mourn\Nwithout ceasing — Odysseus' own son
Dialogue: 1,0:09:24.74,0:09:30.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Telemachus. The gods reared him like a young tree,\Nand I thought he would stand among men no whit behind
Dialogue: 1,0:09:30.52,0:09:36.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}his own dear father, a wonder in build and beauty —\Nand some immortal crippled the balanced mind within him,
Dialogue: 1,0:09:36.58,0:09:42.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}or some man did: he has gone for news of his father\Nto holy Pylos. And the lordly suitors lie in wait
Dialogue: 1,0:09:42.98,0:09:49.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for his homecoming — so that the line of godlike\NArceisius may perish out of Ithaca and leave no name.
Dialogue: 1,0:09:49.66,0:09:55.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Well — we will let him be, whether he is taken or escapes,\Nand the son of Cronos stretches a hand over him.
Dialogue: 1,0:09:55.80,0:10:02.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But come, old man, tell me your own troubles now;\Nand tell me this too, honestly, so that I may know it:
Dialogue: 1,0:10:02.22,0:10:05.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}What man are you, and from where?\NWhere are your city and your parents?
Dialogue: 1,0:10:05.90,0:10:13.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}On what kind of ship did you come? And how did\Nsailors bring you to Ithaca? Who did they claim to be?
Dialogue: 1,0:10:13.02,0:10:15.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}For I hardly think you came here on foot."
Dialogue: 1,0:10:15.80,0:10:18.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:
Dialogue: 1,0:10:18.76,0:10:21.04,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Then I will tell you all of it, and truly.
Dialogue: 1,0:10:21.04,0:10:26.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}If only there were food for the two of us,\Nfor a good while, and sweet wine here inside the hut,
Dialogue: 1,0:10:26.62,0:10:31.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to feast on quietly while others did the work —\Nthen I could go on easily for a full year
Dialogue: 1,0:10:31.86,0:10:37.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and still not finish out the tale of my heart's cares,\Nall that I have labored through by the gods' will.
Dialogue: 1,0:10:37.94,0:10:40.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Out of wide Crete I claim my lineage:
Dialogue: 1,0:10:40.96,0:10:46.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a rich man's son. Many other sons besides\Nwere born and bred in his hall, true-born,
Dialogue: 1,0:10:46.80,0:10:50.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}sons of his wife. My mother was bought — a concubine.
Dialogue: 1,0:10:50.58,0:10:56.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Yet Castor, Hylax' son — the man whose blood I claim —\Nhonored me equally with his lawful sons.
Dialogue: 1,0:10:56.98,0:11:03.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}In those days the Cretan people honored him like a god\Nfor his fortune, his wealth, and his glorious sons.
Dialogue: 1,0:11:03.76,0:11:09.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But the death-spirits came and carried him away\Nto the house of Hades. And his high-hearted sons
Dialogue: 1,0:11:09.44,0:11:15.72,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}divided up the living and cast lots for it,\Nand to me they gave a pittance, and allotted me a house.
Dialogue: 1,0:11:15.72,0:11:22.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Still, I won myself a wife from a family of wide\Nholdings — by my own worth: for I was no empty man,
Dialogue: 1,0:11:22.22,0:11:28.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}no shirker of the fight. Now it is all gone from me;\Nbut even so, I think, looking at the stubble
Dialogue: 1,0:11:28.12,0:11:33.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}you can tell the standing grain. Since then,\Ntrouble past measure has held me.
Dialogue: 1,0:11:33.08,0:11:38.68,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Ares and Athena, yes, they gave me boldness and the\Nstrength that breaks men's ranks. Whenever I picked
Dialogue: 1,0:11:38.68,0:11:45.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the best men for an ambush, sowing evils for the enemy,\Nmy proud heart never once looked forward to death:
Dialogue: 1,0:11:45.30,0:11:48.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}far the first, I would leap out\Nand take my man with the spear —
Dialogue: 1,0:11:48.80,0:11:51.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}whichever of the enemy gave me his back in flight.
Dialogue: 1,0:11:51.42,0:11:57.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Such I was in war. But field-work I never loved,\Nnor the housekeeping that rears shining children:
Dialogue: 1,0:11:57.46,0:12:03.92,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}what I loved, always, was the oared ships,\Nand wars, and polished javelins, and arrows —
Dialogue: 1,0:12:03.92,0:12:06.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}grim things, that other men shudder at.
Dialogue: 1,0:12:06.50,0:12:09.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But I loved what the god, I suppose, set in my heart:
Dialogue: 1,0:12:09.98,0:12:13.04,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}one man delights in one work, another in another.
Dialogue: 1,0:12:13.04,0:12:18.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}For before the sons of the Achaeans set foot on Troy,\Nnine times I led men and fast-running ships
Dialogue: 1,0:12:18.62,0:12:21.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}against foreign peoples, and rich takings came my way.
Dialogue: 1,0:12:21.66,0:12:25.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}From it I drew out my pleasure, and much fell to me
Dialogue: 1,0:12:25.12,0:12:32.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}by lot besides. My house grew great at once,\Nand thereafter I walked among the Cretans feared and respected.
Dialogue: 1,0:12:32.18,0:12:38.06,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But when Zeus of the wide voice devised at last\Nthat hateful road that loosed the knees of many men,
Dialogue: 1,0:12:38.06,0:12:43.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}then they pressed me and glorious Idomeneus to\Nlead the ships to Ilium. There was no device
Dialogue: 1,0:12:43.34,0:12:46.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for refusing: the people's hard talk held us to it.
Dialogue: 1,0:12:46.88,0:12:52.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}There we sons of the Achaeans made war nine years,\Nand in the tenth we sacked Priam's city and set off
Dialogue: 1,0:12:52.54,0:12:55.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}home with our ships; and a god scattered the Achaeans.
Dialogue: 1,0:12:55.94,0:13:00.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But for me — wretched me —\NZeus the deviser designed evils.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:00.26,0:13:05.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}One month, no more, I stayed, delighting in my children,\Nmy wedded wife, and my possessions. And then
Dialogue: 1,0:13:05.90,0:13:10.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}my heart bade me fit out ships and sail\Nfor Egypt, with my godlike companions.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:10.76,0:13:14.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Nine ships I fitted out, and the crews gathered fast.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:14.12,0:13:18.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Six days after that my trusted companions\Nfeasted, and I gave victims in plenty
Dialogue: 1,0:13:18.40,0:13:22.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for sacrifice to the gods,\Nand for the men to make their feast.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:22.22,0:13:27.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}On the seventh we boarded, and from wide Crete\Nwe sailed with a fresh fair North Wind behind us,
Dialogue: 1,0:13:27.62,0:13:32.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}easily, as if downstream. No ship\Nof mine took harm: unhurt and whole
Dialogue: 1,0:13:32.54,0:13:35.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}we sat, and the wind and the\Nsteersmen held them on course.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:35.88,0:13:39.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}On the fifth day we reached the\Nfair-flowing river of Egypt,
Dialogue: 1,0:13:39.36,0:13:42.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and in the river of Egypt I moored my curved ships.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:42.84,0:13:49.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then, you may be sure, I ordered my trusted companions\Nto stay there by the ships and guard the ships,
Dialogue: 1,0:13:49.00,0:13:51.48,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and I sent out scouts to the lookout places.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:51.48,0:13:54.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But they gave way to arrogance —\Nfollowed their own force —
Dialogue: 1,0:13:54.58,0:13:57.04,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and straightway began to pillage the beautiful fields
Dialogue: 1,0:13:57.04,0:14:00.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}of the Egyptian men: they carried\Noff the women and small children,
Dialogue: 1,0:14:00.82,0:14:04.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and killed the men. And quickly the cry reached the city.
Dialogue: 1,0:14:04.32,0:14:06.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Hearing the shout, at the showing of dawn the people
Dialogue: 1,0:14:06.86,0:14:10.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}came; and the whole plain filled with infantry and horses
Dialogue: 1,0:14:10.56,0:14:13.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and the lightning of bronze.\NAnd Zeus who delights in thunder
Dialogue: 1,0:14:13.98,0:14:17.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}threw an evil panic on my companions, and no man dared
Dialogue: 1,0:14:17.60,0:14:20.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}stand and face it: for destruction ringed us everywhere.
Dialogue: 1,0:14:20.88,0:14:23.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}There they killed many of us with the sharp bronze,
Dialogue: 1,0:14:23.76,0:14:28.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and others they led up alive, to slave for them by force.
Dialogue: 1,0:14:28.10,0:14:34.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But as for me — Zeus himself put this thought in\Nmy mind (would I had died instead, and met my doom
Dialogue: 1,0:14:34.32,0:14:37.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}there, in Egypt! for more grief\Nstood waiting to receive me):
Dialogue: 1,0:14:37.88,0:14:41.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}at once I took the well-made helmet from my head,
Dialogue: 1,0:14:41.02,0:14:44.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and the shield from my shoulders,\Nand dropped the spear from my hand,
Dialogue: 1,0:14:44.74,0:14:46.92,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and went straight toward the king's chariot,
Dialogue: 1,0:14:46.92,0:14:51.16,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and clasped his knees and kissed them.\NHe spared me, and had mercy,
Dialogue: 1,0:14:51.16,0:14:54.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and sat me in his car, and took me, weeping, to his house.
Dialogue: 1,0:14:54.84,0:14:59.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Truly, man after man came\Nlunging at me with the ash spear,
Dialogue: 1,0:14:59.12,0:15:02.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}raging to kill me — for they\Nwere angered beyond all measure —
Dialogue: 1,0:15:02.36,0:15:09.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}but he held them off: he feared the wrath of Zeus of\NStrangers, who above all feels fury at evil deeds.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:09.42,0:15:15.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}There I stayed seven years, and I gathered much\Nwealth among the Egyptian men: for all of them gave.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:15.66,0:15:18.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But when the eighth year came wheeling round for me,
Dialogue: 1,0:15:18.30,0:15:22.24,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}then there came a Phoenician,\Na man well-schooled in deceits —
Dialogue: 1,0:15:22.24,0:15:25.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a gnawer of men, who had already done the world much harm.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:25.62,0:15:31.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He talked me round, that one, and took me with him to\NPhoenicia, where his house and his possessions lay,
Dialogue: 1,0:15:31.00,0:15:34.04,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and there I stayed with him for the full round of a year.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:34.04,0:15:37.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But when at last the months and the\Ndays were coming to fulfillment,
Dialogue: 1,0:15:37.34,0:15:43.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}as the year rolled round and the seasons came again,\Nhe put me aboard a seagoing ship for Libya —
Dialogue: 1,0:15:43.20,0:15:46.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a lying scheme: I was to help him run a cargo there,
Dialogue: 1,0:15:46.52,0:15:50.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}but there he meant to sell me, and take a boundless price.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:50.10,0:15:53.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I went with him aboard — suspecting it, but forced.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:53.64,0:15:56.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And she ran on with a fresh fair North Wind behind her,
Dialogue: 1,0:15:56.84,0:16:01.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}on the mid-sea track above Crete.\NAnd Zeus was devising their destruction:
Dialogue: 1,0:16:01.60,0:16:07.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for when we had left Crete behind,\Nand no other land showed anywhere — only sky and sea —
Dialogue: 1,0:16:07.08,0:16:12.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}then the son of Cronos set a dark-blue cloud over\Nthe hollow ship, and the deep went black beneath it.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:12.66,0:16:16.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And Zeus thundered in the same instant,\Nand hurled his bolt into the ship,
Dialogue: 1,0:16:16.60,0:16:20.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and she shivered stem to stern,\Nstruck by the bolt of Zeus,
Dialogue: 1,0:16:20.64,0:16:24.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and filled with the reek of sulfur;\Nand all the men fell from the ship.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:24.14,0:16:26.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Like sea-crows around the black hull
Dialogue: 1,0:16:26.86,0:16:30.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}they were carried off on the waves;\Nand the god took away their homecoming.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:30.94,0:16:34.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But as for me — Zeus himself,\Nthough my heart was full of anguish,
Dialogue: 1,0:16:34.62,0:16:40.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}put the huge mast of the dark-blue-prowed ship into\Nmy two hands, so that I might yet escape the evil.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:40.84,0:16:43.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Wrapped around it I was carried before the deadly winds.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:43.98,0:16:47.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Nine days I was carried;\Nand on the tenth, in the black night,
Dialogue: 1,0:16:47.88,0:16:50.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a great rolling wave brought\Nme near the Thesprotians' land.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:50.82,0:16:57.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}There the king of the Thesprotians, the hero Pheidon,\Ntook me in — and asked no price: his own dear son
Dialogue: 1,0:16:57.90,0:17:01.92,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}had found me beaten down by cold\Nand weariness, and led me home,
Dialogue: 1,0:17:01.92,0:17:05.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}raising me up by the hand,\Nuntil we reached his father's halls;
Dialogue: 1,0:17:05.64,0:17:08.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and he put clothing on me, a cloak and a tunic.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:08.54,0:17:11.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}It was there I heard of Odysseus. For the king said
Dialogue: 1,0:17:11.54,0:17:16.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}he had hosted him, a guest and friend,\Non his way home to his fathers' land,
Dialogue: 1,0:17:16.42,0:17:22.92,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and he showed me all the treasure Odysseus had gathered —\Nbronze and gold and iron worked with labor —
Dialogue: 1,0:17:22.92,0:17:28.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}enough to feed a man's line to the tenth generation,\Nsuch a store of it lay in the lord's halls.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:28.62,0:17:35.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Odysseus, he said, was gone to Dodona, to hear the\Nwill of Zeus out of the god's own high-crowned oak:
Dialogue: 1,0:17:35.14,0:17:41.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}how he should come home to the rich land of Ithaca,\Nopenly or in secret, after so long away.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:41.08,0:17:44.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And he swore an oath to me,\Npouring the libation in his house,
Dialogue: 1,0:17:44.32,0:17:50.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}that a ship was hauled down and a crew standing\Nready to convey him home to his own dear fatherland.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:50.30,0:17:56.92,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But me he sent off first: for a Thesprotian ship\Nhappened to be sailing for Dulichium, rich in wheat.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:56.92,0:18:01.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He charged them to bring me there,\Nwith all care, to King Acastus.
Dialogue: 1,0:18:01.20,0:18:04.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But an evil counsel about me\Npleased their hearts instead —
Dialogue: 1,0:18:04.98,0:18:08.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}so that I might yet come down to the last dregs of misery.
Dialogue: 1,0:18:08.94,0:18:11.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}When the seagoing ship was far out from the land,
Dialogue: 1,0:18:11.82,0:18:15.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}they set about contriving,\Nthen and there, my day of slavery.
Dialogue: 1,0:18:15.88,0:18:22.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}They stripped me of my clothing, cloak and tunic both,\Nand threw around me different gear — this foul rag
Dialogue: 1,0:18:22.42,0:18:26.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and this torn tunic — the very\Nones you see with your own eyes;
Dialogue: 1,0:18:26.98,0:18:31.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and at evening they reached the\Ntilled fields of clear-skied Ithaca.
Dialogue: 1,0:18:31.38,0:18:36.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}There they lashed me tight in the well-benched ship\Nwith a twisted rope, and went ashore themselves
Dialogue: 1,0:18:36.26,0:18:39.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and hurried their supper along the margin of the sea.
Dialogue: 1,0:18:39.46,0:18:44.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But the gods themselves undid my lashing —\Neasily. And wrapping my rag about my head,
Dialogue: 1,0:18:44.76,0:18:50.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I slid down the polished lading-plank, breasted the sea,\Nthen struck out with both arms, swimming,
Dialogue: 1,0:18:50.96,0:18:54.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and very soon I was out of the water, clear of them.
Dialogue: 1,0:18:54.34,0:19:00.72,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I climbed up where a thicket of flowering brush stood,\Nand lay there, crouched. And they, groaning loud,
Dialogue: 1,0:19:00.72,0:19:05.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}cast about; but there seemed no profit for them\Nin searching further, so they went back again
Dialogue: 1,0:19:05.80,0:19:11.24,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}aboard their hollow ship. And the gods hid me easily,\Nand brought me on my way to the steading
Dialogue: 1,0:19:11.24,0:19:15.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}of an understanding man.\NFor it is still my portion to live."
Dialogue: 1,0:19:15.96,0:19:18.72,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:
Dialogue: 1,0:19:18.72,0:19:22.16,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Poor luckless stranger —\Ntruly you have stirred the heart in me,
Dialogue: 1,0:19:22.16,0:19:26.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}telling all this through, all you\Nhave suffered, all your wandering.
Dialogue: 1,0:19:26.12,0:19:29.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But one part is not in order,\Nand you will never persuade me —
Dialogue: 1,0:19:29.74,0:19:35.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}that about Odysseus. Why should a man like you\Nlie into the empty air? I know it well myself,
Dialogue: 1,0:19:35.46,0:19:38.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}my master's homecoming: how the gods all hated him,
Dialogue: 1,0:19:38.26,0:19:41.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}utterly — since they did not\Nbring him down among the Trojans,
Dialogue: 1,0:19:41.14,0:19:45.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}or in the arms of his friends,\Nonce he had wound the war to its end.
Dialogue: 1,0:19:45.34,0:19:48.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then the whole Achaean people would have built him a tomb,
Dialogue: 1,0:19:48.10,0:19:52.06,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and he would have won great fame in\Ntime to come for his son as well.
Dialogue: 1,0:19:52.06,0:19:55.48,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Instead the whirlwinds have\Nsnatched him away without glory.
Dialogue: 1,0:19:55.48,0:20:01.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And I — I keep apart, with my pigs. I do not go to\Nthe city, unless perhaps watchful-minded Penelope
Dialogue: 1,0:20:01.98,0:20:05.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}bids me come, when news arrives from somewhere;
Dialogue: 1,0:20:05.38,0:20:11.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}then they all sit around the newcomer and question\Neverything — those who grieve for their long-gone lord,
Dialogue: 1,0:20:11.02,0:20:14.78,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and those who rejoice, eating up his living unpunished.
Dialogue: 1,0:20:14.78,0:20:20.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But I have no love of asking and inquiring,\Never since an Aetolian cheated me with his tale —
Dialogue: 1,0:20:20.02,0:20:23.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a man who had killed someone,\Nand wandered over the wide earth,
Dialogue: 1,0:20:23.40,0:20:26.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and came to my house; and I received him with open arms.
Dialogue: 1,0:20:26.90,0:20:30.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He said he had seen him among\Nthe Cretans, at Idomeneus' side,
Dialogue: 1,0:20:30.96,0:20:36.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}mending the ships the storms had shattered on him,\Nand said he would come by summer, or by harvest-time,
Dialogue: 1,0:20:36.38,0:20:39.78,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}bringing much wealth,\Ntogether with his godlike companions.
Dialogue: 1,0:20:39.78,0:20:43.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So you, old man of many sorrows —\Nsince some power has brought you to me —
Dialogue: 1,0:20:43.74,0:20:46.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}do not court my favor with lies, do not charm me:
Dialogue: 1,0:20:46.86,0:20:49.92,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}it is not for that I will respect you or befriend you,
Dialogue: 1,0:20:49.92,0:20:52.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And out of pity for yourself."
Dialogue: 1,0:20:52.10,0:20:55.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:
Dialogue: 1,0:20:55.30,0:20:58.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Truly the heart in your breast is past all persuading —
Dialogue: 1,0:20:58.12,0:21:03.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}such a man you are: not even my oath\Nwon you over; nothing convinces you.
Dialogue: 1,0:21:03.32,0:21:06.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Come then, let us make a bargain\Nof it — and above the two of us
Dialogue: 1,0:21:06.46,0:21:10.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}let the gods who hold Olympus stand witness on both sides.
Dialogue: 1,0:21:10.20,0:21:15.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}If your master comes home, to this very house,\Nyou dress me in a cloak and a tunic, and send me on
Dialogue: 1,0:21:15.52,0:21:21.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to Dulichium, where my own heart longs to be;\Nand if your master does not come as I say he will,
Dialogue: 1,0:21:21.00,0:21:24.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}set your slaves on me: pitch me down from the great rock —
Dialogue: 1,0:21:24.30,0:21:28.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}so that the next beggar too may\Nthink twice before he cheats."
Dialogue: 1,0:21:28.02,0:21:30.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And the noble swineherd answered him, and said:
Dialogue: 1,0:21:30.74,0:21:37.16,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Stranger — and a fine name I would win among\Nmankind for goodness, now and ever after,
Dialogue: 1,0:21:37.16,0:21:44.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I who led you to my hut and gave you a guest's due,\Nif then I killed you and took the dear life out of you:
Dialogue: 1,0:21:44.26,0:21:49.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}with a clean heart, after that,\NI could pray to Zeus the son of Cronos!
Dialogue: 1,0:21:49.56,0:21:55.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But it is supper time. Soon now my companions should\Nbe in, so we can make a savory supper in the hut."
Dialogue: 1,0:21:55.98,0:22:01.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So the two of them spoke such things to one another;\Nand the swine came near, and the men who herd them.
Dialogue: 1,0:22:01.90,0:22:04.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}They shut the sows in their pens for the night's sleep,
Dialogue: 1,0:22:04.80,0:22:08.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and a clamor past speech went up as the swine were folded.
Dialogue: 1,0:22:08.18,0:22:11.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then the noble swineherd called to his companions:
Dialogue: 1,0:22:11.10,0:22:14.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Bring the best of the hogs,\Nso I may slaughter it for a guest
Dialogue: 1,0:22:14.26,0:22:17.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}from far away. And we will have some good of it ourselves,
Dialogue: 1,0:22:17.82,0:22:21.24,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}who bear the misery of these\Nwhite-tusked swine and suffer for it
Dialogue: 1,0:22:21.24,0:22:25.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}so long, while others eat our labor and pay nothing."
Dialogue: 1,0:22:25.02,0:22:28.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So speaking, he split the\Nfirewood with the pitiless bronze,
Dialogue: 1,0:22:28.86,0:22:35.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and they brought in a hog, well fattened, five years old,\Nand stood it by the hearth. Nor did the swineherd
Dialogue: 1,0:22:35.30,0:22:38.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}forget the deathless ones: for his mind was set on good.
Dialogue: 1,0:22:38.84,0:22:42.04,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}No — beginning the rite,\Nhe cast into the fire the head-bristles
Dialogue: 1,0:22:42.04,0:22:48.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}of the white-tusked hog, and prayed to all the gods\Nthat wise Odysseus might come home to his own house.
Dialogue: 1,0:22:48.20,0:22:51.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then he lifted an oak billet,\Nleft over from the splitting,
Dialogue: 1,0:22:51.50,0:22:55.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and struck: and the life left the hog;\Nand the others cut the throat,
Dialogue: 1,0:22:55.42,0:22:58.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and singed it, and quickly\Nquartered it out. And the swineherd,
Dialogue: 1,0:22:58.60,0:23:04.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}beginning the offering, laid raw flesh from every\Nlimb in the rich fat, and cast the pieces in the fire,
Dialogue: 1,0:23:04.74,0:23:08.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}sprinkled over with barley meal; and they carved the rest
Dialogue: 1,0:23:08.08,0:23:11.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and threaded it on spits, and roasted it with care,
Dialogue: 1,0:23:11.02,0:23:14.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and drew it all off, and heaped it\Non the platters. And the swineherd
Dialogue: 1,0:23:14.94,0:23:18.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}stood up to divide it: for his mind knew what was fair.
Dialogue: 1,0:23:18.20,0:23:20.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Into seven portions he divided the whole:
Dialogue: 1,0:23:20.54,0:23:23.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}one he set aside, with a prayer,\Nfor the Nymphs and for Hermes,
Dialogue: 1,0:23:23.96,0:23:29.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Maia's son; the rest he served out to each man;\Nand Odysseus he honored with the long unbroken chine
Dialogue: 1,0:23:29.88,0:23:33.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}of the white-tusked hog —\Nand made his master's heart proud.
Dialogue: 1,0:23:33.64,0:23:36.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And Odysseus of the many designs\Nlifted his voice and said:
Dialogue: 1,0:23:36.60,0:23:39.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Eumaeus — may you be as dear to father Zeus
Dialogue: 1,0:23:39.52,0:23:43.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}as you are to me: for you honor such\Na man as I am now with the best."
Dialogue: 1,0:23:43.86,0:23:46.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:
Dialogue: 1,0:23:46.52,0:23:49.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Eat, my god-touched guest,\Nand take your pleasure in such things
Dialogue: 1,0:23:49.64,0:23:53.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}as are here. The god will give\None thing and let another go,
Dialogue: 1,0:23:53.32,0:23:56.78,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}whatever his own heart wills: for he can do all things."
Dialogue: 1,0:23:56.78,0:24:00.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He spoke, and burned the first-cuts\Nto the gods who are forever,
Dialogue: 1,0:24:00.44,0:24:03.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and poured the fire-bright wine,\Nand set the cup in the hands
Dialogue: 1,0:24:03.28,0:24:07.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}of Odysseus, sacker of cities;\Nand sat down to his own portion.
Dialogue: 1,0:24:07.58,0:24:14.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And Mesaulius served them bread — a man the swineherd\Nhad got for himself, alone, while his master was away,
Dialogue: 1,0:24:14.10,0:24:16.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}without his mistress, without old Laertes:
Dialogue: 1,0:24:16.08,0:24:20.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}he had bought him from the Taphians with goods of his own.
Dialogue: 1,0:24:20.26,0:24:23.48,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And their hands went out to the\Ngood things spread before them.
Dialogue: 1,0:24:23.48,0:24:28.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But when they had put away desire for food and drink,\NMesaulius took away the bread; and they, filled
Dialogue: 1,0:24:28.86,0:24:32.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}full of bread and meat, bestirred themselves for bed.
Dialogue: 1,0:24:32.44,0:24:36.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And the night came on, foul,\Nat the moon's dark, and Zeus rained
Dialogue: 1,0:24:36.52,0:24:41.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the whole night through, and the wet\NWest Wind blew big without ceasing.
Dialogue: 1,0:24:41.20,0:24:44.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then Odysseus spoke among them, testing the swineherd —
Dialogue: 1,0:24:44.80,0:24:47.72,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}whether he would strip off his own cloak and give it,
Dialogue: 1,0:24:47.72,0:24:51.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}or urge it out of one of his companions,\Nsince he cared for him so:
Dialogue: 1,0:24:51.98,0:24:55.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Hear me now, Eumaeus, and all you other companions,
Dialogue: 1,0:24:55.30,0:24:58.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}while I speak a word of boasting: it is the crazing wine
Dialogue: 1,0:24:58.74,0:25:02.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}that commands me — wine,\Nthat sets even the deep-minded singing
Dialogue: 1,0:25:02.36,0:25:09.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and giggling softly, and stirs him up to dance,\Nand tosses out many a word better left unspoken.
Dialogue: 1,0:25:09.00,0:25:13.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Still, since I have blurted once, I will hide nothing.
Dialogue: 1,0:25:13.12,0:25:15.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Would I were young, and my strength stood firm in me,
Dialogue: 1,0:25:15.74,0:25:19.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}as on the day we laid our ambush and led it under Troy.
Dialogue: 1,0:25:19.38,0:25:25.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Odysseus led, and Atreus' son Menelaus, and with them\NI was third in command: they asked it themselves.
Dialogue: 1,0:25:25.54,0:25:30.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}When we had come up to the city and the steep wall,\Nwe lay around the town in the dense brushwood,
Dialogue: 1,0:25:30.50,0:25:33.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}crouched under our arms among the reeds and marsh-grass,
Dialogue: 1,0:25:33.40,0:25:37.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and a foul night came on, the North Wind dropping down,
Dialogue: 1,0:25:37.10,0:25:43.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}freezing; and snow came falling on us like hoarfrost,\Nbitter cold, and ice crusted thick on the shields.
Dialogue: 1,0:25:43.80,0:25:46.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then all the others had their cloaks and tunics,
Dialogue: 1,0:25:46.82,0:25:49.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and slept at their ease,\Nshoulders wrapped in their shields;
Dialogue: 1,0:25:49.66,0:25:55.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}but I had left my cloak behind with my companions —\Nfool that I was — never thinking I would freeze:
Dialogue: 1,0:25:55.60,0:25:59.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I had come with only my shield and my shining waist-guard.
Dialogue: 1,0:25:59.12,0:26:02.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But in the night's third watch,\Nwhen the stars had crossed their height,
Dialogue: 1,0:26:02.10,0:26:05.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}then I spoke to Odysseus — he was there beside me —
Dialogue: 1,0:26:05.46,0:26:08.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}nudging him with my elbow; and he listened on the instant:
Dialogue: 1,0:26:08.76,0:26:14.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}'Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices,\NI shall not be among the living long: this cold
Dialogue: 1,0:26:14.82,0:26:21.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}is killing me. I have no cloak. Some power tricked\Nme into a single tunic. And now there is no way out.'
Dialogue: 1,0:26:21.30,0:26:27.48,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So I spoke; and he then held this plan in his heart —\Nsuch a man he was for counsel, and for fighting —
Dialogue: 1,0:26:27.48,0:26:30.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and speaking low, close in my ear, he said:
Dialogue: 1,0:26:30.14,0:26:32.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}'Quiet now, lest some other Achaean hear you.'
Dialogue: 1,0:26:32.58,0:26:35.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then he propped his head on his elbow and spoke out:
Dialogue: 1,0:26:35.30,0:26:38.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}'Hear me, friends. A divine dream came to me in my sleep.
Dialogue: 1,0:26:38.64,0:26:41.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}We have come out too far from the ships. Let someone go
Dialogue: 1,0:26:41.52,0:26:47.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and tell Atreus' son Agamemnon, shepherd of the people,\Nto send more men from the ships to join us here.'
Dialogue: 1,0:26:47.56,0:26:53.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So he spoke; and up jumped Thoas, Andraemon's son,\Nfast, and flung off his purple cloak,
Dialogue: 1,0:26:53.08,0:26:56.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and set off running for the ships.\NAnd I, in that man's garment,
Dialogue: 1,0:26:56.44,0:26:59.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}lay glad until Dawn shone on her golden throne.
Dialogue: 1,0:26:59.86,0:27:02.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Would I were young like that now,\Nand my strength stood firm:
Dialogue: 1,0:27:02.90,0:27:05.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}then someone in this steading\Nmight give a swineherd's cloak —
Dialogue: 1,0:27:05.86,0:27:09.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}both ways at once: in kindness,\Nand in respect for a good man.
Dialogue: 1,0:27:09.94,0:27:13.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}As it is, they scorn me for the foul clothes on my skin."
Dialogue: 1,0:27:13.22,0:27:15.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:
Dialogue: 1,0:27:15.42,0:27:17.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Old man — that is a blameless fable you have told:
Dialogue: 1,0:27:17.98,0:27:21.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}not one word of it off the measure;\Nnothing said without its profit.
Dialogue: 1,0:27:21.40,0:27:28.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So you shall not lack for clothing, nor for anything\Nelse that is owed a hard-worn suppliant when he comes —
Dialogue: 1,0:27:28.22,0:27:32.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}tonight. But at dawn you will knock\Nabout in those rags of yours again:
Dialogue: 1,0:27:32.30,0:27:37.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for there are not many cloaks here, or changes\Nof tunic, to put on — one only for each man.
Dialogue: 1,0:27:37.94,0:27:43.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But when the dear son of Odysseus comes,\Nhe will give you clothing himself, a cloak and a tunic,
Dialogue: 1,0:27:43.54,0:27:46.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and send you wherever your heart and spirit bid you go."
Dialogue: 1,0:27:46.52,0:27:52.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So speaking he sprang up and set a bed for him close\Nby the fire, and threw on it skins of sheep and goats.
Dialogue: 1,0:27:52.86,0:27:56.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}There Odysseus lay down; and over\Nhim the swineherd threw a cloak,
Dialogue: 1,0:27:56.88,0:28:03.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}thick-woven and big, that he kept by him for a change\Nof clothing, to put on when some terrible storm arose.
Dialogue: 1,0:28:03.12,0:28:09.16,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So Odysseus slept there, and beside him slept the\Nyoung men. But the swineherd — a bed in that place
Dialogue: 1,0:28:09.16,0:28:16.24,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}did not please him, to sleep apart from his swine;\Nhe armed himself to go outside. And Odysseus was glad
Dialogue: 1,0:28:16.24,0:28:20.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}that he took such care of his\Nsubstance while he was far away.
Dialogue: 1,0:28:20.66,0:28:24.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}First he slung his sharp sword over his sturdy shoulders,
Dialogue: 1,0:28:24.34,0:28:27.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}then wrapped himself in a\Nwind-breaking cloak, thick-woven,
Dialogue: 1,0:28:27.98,0:28:34.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and took up the fleece of a big well-fattened goat,\Nand took his sharp javelin, to hold off dogs and men,
Dialogue: 1,0:28:34.08,0:28:40.25,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and went out to lie where the white-tusked boars slept,\Nunder a hollow rock, in the lee of the North Wind.
